    "blocks": [
      {"id":"title","type":"markdown","layout":"full","body":"# Independent-Model Broad-Corpus Split — Iteration 022"},
      {"id":"summary","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"model_split_results","body":"## Technical summary\n\n**Parley preserved final correctness but did not reach strict parity under the independent model.** All three languages passed 72/72 hidden tasks. Parley passed 39/72 on the first check and used a median **13.04k reported tokens per task**, versus Python's 4.08k and Rust's 7.27k. It passed only correctness, so the frozen gate finished 1/4."},
      {"id":"metrics","type":"metric-strip","layout":"full","cardIds":["sessions_card","tasks_card","hidden_card","gate_card","first_card","membership_card","multiple_card","number_card"]},
      {"id":"scope","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"model_split_results","body":"## What this split actually tests\n\nIteration 022 holds tasks, prompts, hidden cases, runner, and the 1,519-character Parley instruction fixed while switching all three languages to `gpt-5.6-terra`. Parley is v0.3.155, including contextual value identifier `number`; iteration 021 used `gpt-5.6-sol` and Parley v0.3.154. The primary evidence is therefore the within-022 language comparison. Cross-iteration movement mixes a model change with a compiler change and cannot identify either effect alone."},
      {"id":"effort_text","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"model_split_results","body":"## Agent effort remains far above both baselines\n\nParley's 13.04k median reported tokens per task was **3.20× Python** and **1.80× Rust**. Median elapsed time was 9.50 seconds per task versus 3.67 and 6.24. Parley needed 15 repair turns; Rust needed six; Python needed none. Every Parley session exceeded its Python counterpart, and five of six exceeded Rust."},
      {"id":"token_chart_block","type":"chart","chartId":"token_chart","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"session_chart_block","type":"chart","chartId":"session_chart","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"reliability_text","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"model_split_results","body":"## Repairs recovered complete correctness\n\nFinal hidden judgment was perfect across all 216 assignments. First-check reliability was 54.17% for Parley, 100% for Python, and 88.89% for Rust. No Parley session passed all twelve tasks on its first check, so the preregistered repair-free sensitivity analysis is unavailable."},
      {"id":"reliability_chart_block","type":"chart","chartId":"reliability_chart","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"task_chart_block","type":"chart","chartId":"task_chart","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"failure_text","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"model_split_results","body":"## The only independent cross-task signature is a redundant word in map membership\n\nFifteen failures across inventory ledger, sparse dot product, and word frequency used forms such as `balances contains key name`. They appeared in five independent sessions. Supported Parley already expresses the same operation as `balances contains name`; the skill's schematic `map contains key` uses `map` and `key` as placeholders, not literal grammar. This is real evidence about agent expectation, but not evidence from a new corpus."},
      {"id":"signature_chart_block","type":"chart","chartId":"signature_chart","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"boundary","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"model_split_results","body":"## No compiler change is authorized by iteration 022\n\nThe corpus was deliberately reused for an independent-model split, and its preregistered stop rule forbids learning ergonomics from it after output. Adding literal `key` would also duplicate an existing, semantically complete membership operation solely to match these transcripts. The other signatures are weaker: seven unwrapped-input failures came from one session; bare `nothing` is one task; `repeat while` came from one session and duplicates `while`; the remaining expression, midpoint, and algorithm failures are isolated. None advances."},
      {"id":"number_result","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"model_split_results","body":"## Contextual `number` replay signal is clean, but not causal\n\nThe exact reserved-name P209 signature fell from ten events in iteration 021 to **zero** here. That is consistent with v0.3.155 accepting value-level `number`, and the ten frozen sources already passed 50/50 exact replay cases. Because the model also changed, iteration 022 cannot estimate how much of the first-check or token movement came from the compiler change."},
      {"id":"model_table_block","type":"table","tableId":"model_table","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"source_text","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"model_split_results","body":"## Generated source remains shorter than Rust\n\nParley's median generated program used about 142 rough source tokens per task, versus Python's 105 and Rust's 212. Parley source was therefore 33.18% shorter than Rust while agent effort was 79.5% higher. Source verbosity alone does not explain the efficiency gap; failed first checks and repeated session context dominate."},
      {"id":"source_chart_block","type":"chart","chartId":"source_chart","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"language_table_block","type":"table","tableId":"language_table","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"task_table_block","type":"table","tableId":"task_table","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"signature_table_block","type":"table","tableId":"signature_table","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"audit_table_block","type":"table","tableId":"audit_table","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"session_table_block","type":"table","tableId":"session_table","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"methodology","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"model_split_results","body":"## Frozen methodology\n\n- **Matrix:** 18 fresh sessions, twelve tasks, three languages, six complete-bundle replicates, seed `20260808`.\n- **Toolchain:** Parley 0.3.155 at preregistration commit `5b407e7`, `gpt-5.6-terra` medium, Codex CLI 0.146.0.\n- **Instruction:** unchanged 1,519-character skill, SHA `6ca098e4…`; the one allowed compression experiment remains closed.\n- **Protocol:** no workspace inspection; first action creates solutions; exact `./check` only; hidden cases withheld.\n- **Integrity:** 18 unique thread IDs, 18/18 checker integrity and protocol compliance, no timeouts, runner errors, or nonzero agent exits.\n- **Hashes:** task manifest `45f6387f…`; protocol `8aea92b1…`; frozen result `8594f5e8…`."},
      {"id":"limitations","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"model_split_results","body":"## Limitations and uncertainty\n\nSix replicates establish direction, not a population estimate across models or prompt styles. The corpus is reused and all tasks are compact stdin/stdout programs; it does not test modules, files, records, packages, concurrency, or long-lived applications. Reported tokens include repeated cached/context input, not only visible output. Rough source tokens are a lexical proxy. A single session can repeat one authoring habit across many tasks, which is why session independence is reported alongside event counts."},
      {"id":"decision","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"model_split_results","body":"## Decision and next step\n\n1. Preserve iteration 022 unchanged as a failed 1/4 parity result with complete correctness.\n2. Make no compiler, grammar, runtime, prompt, or skill change from this reused corpus.\n3. Keep report 013 and every later numbered report immutable; preserve raw evidence and hashes.\n4. Move to a preregistered, previously unused application-style corpus that tests general completeness—modules, records, files, packages, and multi-function programs—rather than another syntax spelling diagnostic.\n5. Judge any future change by independent new-task recurrence, general usefulness, semantic consistency, and maintainability."},
      {"id":"questions","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"model_split_results","body":"## Further questions\n\n- Does the efficiency gap persist on application-style work where Parley's concise source can amortize fixed context?\n- Which failures recur across unrelated new tasks and independent sessions, not merely within one bundle?\n- Can stable compiler diagnostics and existing canonical operations reduce repair effort without expanding syntax?"}
    ]
  },
